  "query_preview": "..CORRECTED the right long term section / Friday \nthrough Monday/...Ensemble models agreeing on an upper level ridge\ndissipating and being replaced by a weak trough over the southern\npanhandle by Friday, with the ridge rebuilding early next week. \nThis shift will bring positive vorticity advection and some \nlifting, while moisture advection arrives via an easterly wave in \nthe 700mb level. Additionally, a surface maritime ridge forming in\nthe eastern gulf will generate northwesterly winds across the \npanhandle which could work in conjunction with diurnally driven \nsea breezes to promote weak onshore flow toward the end of the \nweek.\n\nRegarding severe weather potential for end of week/early weekend, \nguidance is keying in on seasonably high CAPE values in parts of \nthe central and southern regions, though forecaster confidence \nremains low due to drier conditions in many of the atmospheric\npressure levels and a lack of support for sustaining storms \nmoving in from Canada. Cumulus fields are more likely than storms,\nas a lack of a cap and significant moisture inhibits potential \nconvection. While westerly flow on Friday might drive some \nlocalized convection through upsloping, marine influences will \nlikely stabilize the surface layer and choke off development. \nMeanwhile, light winds and clearing skies are expected to boost \ntemperatures, with Friday and Saturday continuing this warm spell \nas temperatures reach the upper 70s to low 80s, before cooling \nreturns Sunday into Monday as the surface high pressure weakens. \nThere is still a lot of spread in the track of a low pressure \ncenter moving into the central gulf next week, and if lingering \nhigh pressure will block precipitation from moving in. We will \ncontinue to monitor this next system and associated rainfall \naccumulation.",
